laurel 2700: vibration on the Mi A3. PMI632 LDO vibrator (ERM, SPMI SID 3, base 0x5700, 3000 mV) over an own SPMI lane (APID of PPID 0x357, EE 0 owner check; observer EXT_READL + rw-channel EXT_WRITEL; no state shared with sweet_spmi.c). Motor thread: overdrive start 3544 mV / 30 ms (Qualcomm default, kill switch laurel_haptic_od=0), minimum on-time 30 ms (50 ms without overdrive), duty limit (heat budget: 70 % sustainable, cut at 4000 ms, resume at 2000 ms), watchdog thread call (motor on + thread stalled 1 s = off). iOS: "vibrator" IOService (LaurelVibrator.cpp, port of negroni 0301: FigVibrator hertz_millisecs/repeat/stop) and /dev/redwood-haptic (topaz audio_plugin.c Actuator samples, S16LE mono 48 kHz -> 2 ms blocks -> on-windows). IODeviceTree:/product/haptics closed-loop = 1 (negroni 0302) only when the root has RedwoodAudio.driver + VirtualAudio.plugin. Self-check at start: 'LAUREL HAPTIC: on: PMI632 LDO vib SID3 0x5700 3000 mV (...)' or 'LAUREL HAPTIC: off: <why>'; 'LAUREL HAPTIC UI: on|off: <why>'. Repeat bounds (2026-11-01, endless vibration: iOS FigVibrator loops {[on, off], repeat} until its client stops it; a dictionary without TotalDuration gets a 31-day total): a repeat stops 60 s after its request when no new request comes (kill switch laurel_vib_cap=0 = the old 5 min), and a power/volume key press stops a running repeat (sweet_keys.c -> laurel_vibrator_key). Each request shape is logged once with the caller ('LAUREL VIBRATOR: request kind N from PROC: SHAPE'). Kill switch laurel_haptic=0, laurel_haptic_ui=0. Details: docs/laurel_sprout/haptics.md.

