--- a/iokit/Kernel/arm/LaurelVibrator.cpp	1970-01-01 05:30:00.000000000 +0530
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/arm/LaurelVibrator.cpp
@@ -0,0 +1,433 @@
+/*
+ * laurel_sprout (Xiaomi Mi A3): the legacy iOS "vibrator" IOService (laurel 2700, port of negroni 0301)
+ * on the PMI632 LDO ERM motor of bsd/dev/laurel_haptic.c, and the closed-loop node (negroni 0302).
+ *
+ * iOS 16 20H392 [V negroni docs/haptics-plan.md section 4, shared cache]: MediaExperience FigVibrator,
+ * when VibeSynthEngineHasHardwareSupport() is false (closed-loop MobileGestalt answer false), takes
+ * IOServiceGetMatchingService(IOServiceNameMatching("vibrator")) and IORegistryEntrySetCFProperties:
+ *   start:   { "hertz_millisecs" = ( {"intensity" = SInt32 16.16}, on_ms, false, off_ms, ... ), "repeat" = bool }
+ *   stop:    a bare CFNumber 0
+ * No user client. Here: pairs (state, ms); state = a dictionary with "intensity" > 0 or true = on,
+ * false or intensity 0 = off (an ERM on an LDO has no amplitude control worth using: lower voltages
+ * do not start it reliably). The steps run on our work loop timer; an on step asks
+ * laurel_haptic_vib(ms + 10) so back-to-back on steps do not gap.
+ * laurel_haptic.c adds the minimum on-time, the overdrive start and the duty limit.
+ *
+ * Repeat (2026-11-01, endless vibration on the first SpringBoard boot) [V 20H392 MediaExperience,
+ * unslid]: FigVibrator's play (0x195a42a48) sends {[on, off], repeat = true} when the total is longer
+ * than one period, and the only end is a one-shot dispatch timer at the total (0x195a42fc0..0x195a43004,
+ * handler 0x195a43b34 sends the CFNumber 0). For a pattern dictionary without TotalDuration the caller
+ * (0x195a94448) sets the total to 2678400 s (31 days): the loop ends only when the iOS client calls
+ * FigVibratorStop (0x195a940dc). On the phone one such start came (400 ms on / 100 ms off, repeat 1)
+ * and no request after it, so the motor ran until the reboot. Two bounds, both features of this file:
+ *   - a repeating pattern stops LV_REPEAT_CAP_MS (60 s) after its start request when no new request
+ *     comes. 60 s: an incoming call rings at most about 30 s before the network diverts it (3GPP
+ *     no-reply timer 5..30 s), the Emergency SOS countdown is shorter; 60 s is 2x that margin [I].
+ *     Kill switch laurel_vib_cap=0 (then the old 5 min bound).
+ *   - a power or volume key press (bsd/dev/sweet_keys.c -> laurel_vibrator_key) stops a running
+ *     repeat, as iOS silences a ring with these keys. One-shot patterns are not touched.
+ * Each new request kind (its shape: classes, keys, element types) is logged once with the caller
+ * process, so the next boot shows what iOS sends.
+ *
+ * laurel_haptic_publish_closed_loop(): IODeviceTree:/product/haptics {closed-loop 1,
+ * supports-3rd-party-haptics 1} (D22 ADT values). Called by laurel_haptic.c only when the root has the
+ * RedwoodAudio.driver HAL plug-in (its "Actuator" -> /dev/redwood-haptic) and VirtualAudio.plugin:
+ * with closed-loop on, FigVibrator, CoreHaptics and UIKit all play on VirtualAudioDevice_Actuator.
+ * Not in scripts/laurel/mkadt.py on purpose: a static node would turn every vibration off on a root
+ * without the plug-in.
+ */
+#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET)
